NNAAMMEE
     ggeeoomm — universal control utility for GEOM classes

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ggeeoomm _c_l_a_s_s hheellpp
     ggeeoomm _c_l_a_s_s lliisstt [--aa] [_n_a_m_e _._._.]
     ggeeoomm _c_l_a_s_s ssttaattuuss [--aaggss] [_n_a_m_e _._._.]
     ggeeoomm _c_l_a_s_s llooaadd [--vv]
     ggeeoomm _c_l_a_s_s uunnllooaadd [--vv]
     ggeeoomm --pp _p_r_o_v_i_d_e_r_-_n_a_m_e
     ggeeoomm --tt

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggeeoomm utility is used to control various GEOM classes.  A class has to
     be aware of ggeeoomm communication methods, but there are also some standard
     commands which can be used for existing ggeeoomm unaware classes.  Here is
     the list of standard commands:

     hheellpp    List all available commands for the given class.

     lliisstt    Print detailed information (within the given class) about all ge‐
             oms (if no additional arguments were specified) or the given ge‐
             oms.  This command is only available if the given class exists in
             the kernel.  Additional options include:

             --aa  Print information for geoms without providers.

     ssttaattuuss  Print general information (within the given class) about all ge‐
             oms (if no additional arguments were specified) or the given ge‐
             oms.  This command is only available if the given class exists in
             the kernel.

             Additional options include:

             --aa  When used with --gg, print status for geoms without providers.

             --gg  Report statuses for geoms instead of providers.

             --ss  Produce script-friendly output.

     llooaadd    Load the kernel module that implements the given class.  This
             command is only available if the class does not yet exist in the
             kernel and the file _g_e_o_m__⟨_c_l_a_s_s⟩_._k_o can be found in one of the
             directories specified in _k_e_r_n_._m_o_d_u_l_e___p_a_t_h sysctl.

     uunnllooaadd  Unload the kernel module which implements the given class.  This
             command is only available if the given class is loaded as a ker‐
             nel module.

     Additional options include:

     --pp _p_r_o_v_i_d_e_r_-_n_a_m_e
             Print detailed information about the geom which provides
             _p_r_o_v_i_d_e_r_-_n_a_m_e.

     --tt      Display geoms hierarchy as a tree.

     Class-specific commands are implemented as shared libraries which are
     stored in _/_l_i_b_/_g_e_o_m_/ directory and are loaded via dlopen(3) function when
     the class name is known.  When a class-specific shared library exists, a
     direct utility should also be available under the name of gg_c_l_a_s_s.

     Currently, classes aware of ggeeoomm are:

           ••   CACHE
           ••   CONCAT
           ••   ELI
           ••   JOURNAL
           ••   LABEL
           ••   MIRROR
           ••   MOUNTVER
           ••   MULTIPATH
           ••   NOP
           ••   PART
           ••   RAID
           ••   RAID3
           ••   SCHED
           ••   SHSEC
           ••   STRIPE
           ••   UNION
           ••   VINUM (deprecated)
           ••   VIRSTOR

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The following environment variables affect the execution of ggeeoomm:

     GEOM_LIBRARY_PATH  Specifies the path where shared libraries are stored
                        instead of _/_l_i_b_/_g_e_o_m_/.  Multiple paths can be speci‐
                        fied with a colon-separated list of paths.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     Exit status is 0 on success, and 1 if the command fails.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following example shows how to set up a stripe on three disks for au‐
     tomatic configuration:

           geom stripe label -v -s 65536 data /dev/da0 /dev/da1 /dev/da2

     or:

           gstripe label -v -s 65536 data /dev/da0 /dev/da1 /dev/da2

     Print the list of all providers from the DISK class:

           geom disk list

     Unload a kernel module which implements the MD class:

           geom md unload

